==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-au-hang out to dry.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|hang<,,hung> out to dry}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|idiomatic}} To [[abandon]] someone who is in [[need]] or in [[danger]], especially a colleague or one dependent.
#: {{syn|en|leave someone hanging}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1989|author=w:Kurt Cobain|title={{w|About a Girl (Nirvana song)|About a Girl}}|artist={{w|Nirvana (band)|Nirvana}}|album={{w|Bleach (Nirvana album)|Bleach}}|passage=I'll take advantage while / You '''hang''' me '''out to dry'''}}
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|date=March 7 2008|author=Joe Fiorito|url=http://www.thestar.com/printArticle/310368|title=Our home-care system '''hangs''' families '''out to dry'''|work=Toronto Star|accessdate=23 August 2008|passage=Without supplemental health insurance, we would have been '''hung out to dry'''.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20081202175321/http://www.thestar.com/printArticle/310368|archivedate=2 December 2008}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Clare McNeil|title=Don't hang young people out to dry|work=The Guardian|date=2011-01-25|url=https://www.theguardian.com/society/joepublic/2011/jan/25/high-youth-unemployment-young-people-hung-out-to-dry|issn=0261-3077|passage=It's hard to avoid the conclusion that young people are being '''hung out to dry'''.}}
# {{&lit|en|hang|out|dry}}

====Usage notes====
* Often carries an element of betrayal of someone who might have expected protection or assistance.
* The phrase paints a vivid picture of isolation and vulnerability.
* Journalists might use the term to describe public figures or entities that have been abandoned by their allies or supporters.

